//? Title: Flow Matching: Straight Couplings and Deterministic Velocity Paths
//? Subtitle: Native tensor components with editable training and sampling loops
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
This report preserves the original flow_matching experiment and moves reusable tensor operations into datamunge. Parameter stores, objectives, minibatch loops, optimizer writes and sampling trajectories are visible in the complete program.
)
\toc

> Method, preserved protocol and observed limitations

[Lipman et al., Flow Matching for Generative Modeling (2022)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.02747) motivates learning a velocity field through conditional paths. The original experiment independently couples base Gaussian z with a noisy eight-mode ring endpoint x. At time t the location is(1-t)*z+t*x and the regression target is x-z. A3-to 32-to 32-to 2 SiLU MLP has 1250 values. The caller performs48 Adam writes across three epochs.

The deployed velocity is integrated deterministically from time 0 to 1 using Euler and RK4 at 1/8/16/32 steps from 1024 common Gaussian starts. Every stage and full2x2 step Jacobian is retained. A separate analytic eight-Gaussian-mixture velocity supplies a refinement control. The source's local Jacobian probes may extrapolate beyond time 1; the native API preserves that explicit diagnostic rather than clipping time.

Test velocity MSE is 2.936365366, versus 2.983879717 for zero velocity,2.417004802 for a fitting-only Gaussian and 2.096333981 for the analytic conditional velocity. This short learned fit does not beat those baselines. A single Euler step is not guaranteed to map the base distribution to the target ring.

> Follow the state through one model operation

The method diagram separates the explicitly supplied state and conditioning from the predictor and the single transition. The caller owns repeated transitions and training.

